    "thinking": "We are asked to propose a bounded HPC source-code experiment. The goal is \"memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking\". We have the current source code for Problem B (Submatrix of Maximum Sum). The baseline uses a sliding window technique with snake-like traversal (alternating left-to-right then right-to-left) to compute sums incrementally.\n\nWe need to propose one candidate relevant to our role. Our role is A, which focuses on memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking. We have previous experiments: e2410045687645ff reduced tile size from 256 to 128 and precomputed row offsets; it scored ~0.6914 (mean time ~0.06006). Another experiment 1b2f555e6e184fc8 used 2D tiling with block size 64, scoring ~0.6855 (time ~0.06175). The best so far is around 0.69.\n\nWe need to propose a new transformation that improves memory locality further. Looking at the current code: it uses snake traversal which has good cache behavior for column-wise updates? Actually, when moving horizontally, we subtract/add columns; each column access involves striding across rows (k loop). That's not ideal because matrix is row-major. When moving vertically (changing rows), we add/subtract entire rows, which are contiguous in memory.\n\nThe snake traversal alternates direction to reuse the sum from previous submatrix. However, the inner loops for column updates access non-contiguous elements: `sum -= matrix[k+i][exitingCol]` where k varies over s and i is fixed row offset; this accesses a column slice (stride n). That's poor spatial locality.\n\nWe could improve by changing data layout to store columns contiguously? But that would require transposing or using a different storage order. Alternatively, we can restructure the algorithm to use row-major access patterns: instead of sliding window with snake traversal, we could compute submatrix sums using prefix sums (integral image). That is O(N^2) after precomputation and then constant time per submatrix sum. But that would be a different algorithm; however, it might be allowed as long as correctness holds.\n\nBut the problem statement says \"You can modify the program in any way you see fit\". So we could implement integral image approach: compute prefix sums P where P[i][j] = sum of matrix[0..i-1][0..j-1]. Then submatrix sum from (r,c) to (r+S-1, c+S-1) is P[r+S][c+S] - P[r][c+S] - P[r+S][c] + P[r][c]. That would be O(N^2) for prefix sums and then O((N-S+1)^2) constant-time queries. But we need to find max sum, so we iterate over all top-left corners.\n\nThat approach has excellent memory locality: building prefix sums accesses matrix row-major; querying submatrix sums also accesses prefix array in a regular pattern (four lookups per corner). However, it uses more memory (prefix sums of same size as matrix) and may have higher arithmetic intensity but less cache misses. Given N up to 8000, matrix is ~64 million ints (~256 MB if using int? Actually each row has N ints, total N^2 = 64e6, at 4 bytes = 244 MB). That's large; prefix sums would double memory usage (488 MB) which might be too high for some systems. But the problem constraints: N < 8000, so max N=7999 => ~63.98 million ints per matrix, ~256 MB. Two such matrices would be >512 MB, possibly exceeding available memory? The baseline uses only one matrix (plus sliding window sum). So integral image might cause memory issues.\n\nAlternatively, we can improve the existing snake algorithm by blocking: process multiple rows at once to reuse column sums across adjacent submatrices in vertical direction. But that's similar to previous 2D tiling experiment.\n\nAnother idea: change loop order for column updates to access contiguous elements? Actually, when moving horizontally, we need to subtract a column and add the next column. The column is accessed with stride n (row-major). To improve locality, we could store columns as separate arrays or use a transposed view. But that would require extra memory.\n\nMaybe we can precom