so, the current code uses std::vector of vectors which has indirection overhead. We could flatten matrix into a single vector<int> with row-major indexing: index = i*n + j. That improves memory locality for all accesses because data is contiguous.\n\nLet's check previous experiments: Did any use flattened array? The baseline uses vector<vector>. Our experiment e2410045687645ff reduced tile size and precomputed offsets; it likely kept the same structure. Experiment 1b2f555e6e184fc8 used 2D tiling with block size 64, also probably using vector of vectors.\n\nFlattening to a single contiguous array would improve cache performance for row accesses (which are already good) and column accesses (still strided but at least no double indirection). Also, we can use pointer arithmetic instead of indexing.\n\nAdditionally, we can change the snake traversal to always move left-to-right? Actually, snake is used to reuse sum when moving vertically: after finishing a row, you go down one row and reverse direction. That's fine.\n\nWe could also try using OpenMP parallelization? But the problem expects serial solution? The baseline is sequential; we can use parallelism but it might be considered cheating? The rules allow any modification. However, our role A focuses on memory locality etc., not parallelism per se. But we could add OpenMP to speed up loops like initial matrix reading and prefix sum computation if we go that route.\n\nGiven the constraints (4 cores), parallelization could give significant speedup. But previous experiments didn't use it; maybe they are focusing on serial optimization. The goal is \"memory locality, loop order, data layout and blocking\", so parallelism might be out of scope. However, we can still propose a transformation that improves memory locality without explicit threading.\n\nLet's think about the most impactful change: flattening matrix to 1D array and using row-major access for column updates? Actually, when updating columns in snake traversal, w